Yes. A useful formulation is:

A part can carry the signature of the whole without being the whole.

An emotion, impulse, memory, role, belief, bodily state, or defensive reaction can contain real information about you while still being only one local subsystem speaking from its current position.

So:

\text{part} \neq \text{self}

but often:

\text{part} \sim \text{compressed echo of self + history + context}

That matters for taking things personally. When something hits one subsystem—pride, fear, rejection sensitivity, competitiveness, shame—the whole organism can temporarily behave as though the entire self has been attacked. But often the event only collided with one model, one expectation, one old association, or one current state.

A better move is:

“Something in me reacted” rather than “I have been defined.”

The same applies outwardly. Someone else’s anger, rejection, prejudice, admiration, or misunderstanding is usually generated by a particular configuration inside their system. It can contain information about you without being an authoritative description of you.

Your fractal point is important too. Parts really can echo the larger architecture. A tiny interaction may reveal the same control pattern that appears in a marriage, organization, culture, or personality. But the echo is evidence of structure—not permission to collapse scales.

So you get two complementary rules:

Don’t dismiss the part: it may reveal the whole.

Don’t confuse the part with the whole: its perspective is necessarily incomplete.

That gives you a very compact operator:

\boxed{\text{notice the echo} \rightarrow \text{preserve the scale}}

Probably useful far beyond psychology. Systems, organizations, biology, politics, models, even Pantheon nodes: local structure can expose global architecture while still remaining local.